WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:06.020
In the summer of 1954, the Yangtze River flooded.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:11.130
At least 30,000 people were killed in one
of the deadliest disasters of its kind in

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.480
history.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.880
The timing — just five years after the founding
of the People’s Republic of China — embedded

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.330
in the tragedy an additional layer of meaning.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:27.259
For the superstitious, it was not a cheerful
start to the “Revolutionary Cause”.

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:32.689
But rather than let this become a stain on
the new nation, Mao seized the opportunity

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:38.700
to assert the party’s resilience and depict
nature as a common enemy to be dominated,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.340
overcome, and, ultimately, conquered.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:48.730
Two years after the floods, Mao swam the Yangtze
in a triumph of propaganda.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:53.760
To commemorate this moment, the chairman wrote
a poem in which he dreamed of constructing

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.720
a “Wall of Stone” across the water so
great it would impress even a mountain goddess.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.230
China’s war on water had begun.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:08.810
The practical success of the many dams and
dikes built during the Mao era was mixed,

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.470
but the political success was unquestionable.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:17.430
Rivers are said to be the lifeblood of a nation,
and the myths surrounding them are ripe for

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.990
nation-building.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:25.000
The Yellow and Yangtze rivers, in particular,
are of special historical significance.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.170
The former, whose taming was arguably the
foundation of early government, is considered

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.560
the “birthplace of China” and holds over
40% of the country’s farmland.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.530
Mao’s preferred design for the new national
flag featured only a single star and horizontal

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.520
line representing the Yellow river.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:50.650
The Yangtze river, meanwhile, is pictured
on banknotes and is the center of rice production,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.310
textiles, and much of the nation’s industry.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:02:00.659
Control of water has long meant practical
control of food, trade, energy, transportation,

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:02.760
and protection against invaders.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.959
Daming these ancient waterways was therefore
just as much a testament to China’s strength

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:12.859
as it was a means of managing water.

00:02:12.859 --> 00:02:18.040
Engineering the environment led to the perception
of China — and, more specifically, the party

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:24.180
— as a force greater than nature — one
strong, capable, and prudent enough to build

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.920
something so colossal.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:32.919
It was in this same light that, ten years
after his first swim in the Yangtze, Mao returned

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.969
to affirm his grip on power.

00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:42.260
Now 72 years old and facing a flurry of rumors
about his health, he once again used his association

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.169
with the river to demonstrate vitality.

00:02:45.169 --> 00:02:59.689
Accompanied by six floating bodyguards, Mao
swam a whole 9 miles according to state propaganda.

00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:05.650
And in 2003, 47 years later, Mao’s “Wall
of Stone” was realized.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:12.459
Indeed, in the Three Gorges Dam, the mighty
Yangtze was finally conquered.

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:15.680
Water spawned Chinese civilization.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.650
Control of water gave and later snatched away
power from Imperial dynasties.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:26.230
And the state’s engineering dominance over
water showed the world what it was capable

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:27.450
of.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:33.949
It is thus rather poetic that someday very
soon, water will once again play a defining

00:03:33.949 --> 00:03:36.719
role in China’s future.

00:03:36.719 --> 00:03:42.549
Rather than suffering from too much water,
as in the floods of decades past, today’s

00:03:42.549 --> 00:03:46.560
China faces a different kind of challenge:
not enough of it.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.510
And it will severely inhibit the country’s
ambitions.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:58.159
In the past 20 years, 28,000 of its rivers
have disappeared — equivalent to the entire

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:01.049
basin area of the Mississippi.

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:07.189
The flow of the Yellow River is now just 10%
of what it was in the 1940s, making it the

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:10.359
most threatened river in all of Asia.

00:04:10.359 --> 00:04:13.169
The number of dry-ups has increased rapidly.

00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:16.459
And that’s just the beginning.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:18.650
But don’t just take my word.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:19.980
Take China’s...

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:25.290
Its leaders have called the water shortage
a “threat to the survival of the Chinese

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:31.440
nation”, announced the need to “fight
for every drop of water or die”, and its

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:38.180
own Ministry of Water Resources predicts a
serious water crisis by 2030.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.980
Sponsored by Brilliant.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:46.450
Learn math, science, and computer science
intuitively with the link in the description.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:51.380
The problem facing China is not, exactly,
that it lacks water.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:54.910
Make no mistake: China has a lot.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:05:01.030
Its freshwater resources are the 5th highest
in the world — behind only Brazil, Russia,

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:07.100
the U.S., and Canada — two of which have
the unfair advantage of lying in Polar regions.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:12.700
And measured by the length of all navigable
waterways, China is naturally blessed with

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:18.590
the number one position — more than twice
all of Europe combined, in fact.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:23.350
The problem slightly peaks its head out when
we adjust for population.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:30.610
Per Capita, China’s freshwater ranking falls
from 5th to 105th — something like 2,000

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.950
cubic meters to the world average of 8,000.

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:39.590
Still, there are many large and even rich
countries lower down on the list.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:46.030
China’s per capita resources are only modestly
lower than the UK’s, and significantly higher

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:47.940
than Germany’s.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.920
One of the many reasons China’s water scarcity
receives equally scarce attention is that

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.770
it’s well hidden in international comparisons.

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:03.080
Only when we ask where its water is located,
do we really begin to understand the issue.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:09.010
When it comes to water, China is split in
two, and the Yangtze is an inexact but useful

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.430
artificial dividing line.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.580
To the South, the theme is wet.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.320
These subtropical latitudes receive lots and
lots of rain.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:24.430
Average annual rainfall in the region is over
2,000 millimeters, most of which occurs in

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:25.580
the summer.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:31.300
It should be no surprise then that the staple
food here is rice, one of the most water-intensive

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:32.610
crops.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:37.040
To the north of the Yangtze, think cold and
dry.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:43.410
This is a temperate-to-arid region with only
about 200-400 millimeters of annual rainfall.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.820
There’s some rain, but not much.

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:51.950
Just barely enough to sustain agriculture,
and noodles are the staple.

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:56.950
The problem is not just where but also when
the water is located.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:02.710
Even what little precipitation the North does
receive, it usually comes all at once, making

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:06.520
it even less useful than these raw numbers
imply.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:11.730
The same farm might contend with both seasonal
droughts in the crucial Spring months, followed

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.950
by monsoon floods in July and August.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:21.320
And when one area experiences one, other areas
are likely to simultaneously experience the

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:22.690
other.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:27.830
Lack of water increases the concentration
of salt in the soil, and the hot summer months

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:30.780
then pull those salts to the surface.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:36.990
Irregular and unpredictable weather makes
planning for these extremes difficult-to-impossible.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:42.040
And to make matters worse, climate change
is aggravating regional differences — making

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:46.500
the South even wetter and the North even dryer.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:51.400
This North-South divide is the fundamental
challenge facing China.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:55.680
Lots of water below the Yangtze; not enough
above it.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:01.720
Water, of course, is renewed in a cycle, but
natural laws do not obey the economic laws

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.950
of supply and demand.

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:10.740
The two halves of China share almost equal
portions of GDP, industrial output, and population.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:16.860
Except: the North holds only 20% of its water,
and the South, 80.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:22.080
You might assume that, given this disparity,
agriculture would be mainly concentrated in

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:23.200
the South.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:24.920
But not so.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:32.320
64% of total arable land and some 40% of farmland
is located in the inhospitable and highly

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.469
temperamental North.

00:08:34.469 --> 00:08:40.120
Put differently, the amount of freshwater
per unit of farmland in the North China plain

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.159
is 15% of the country’s average.

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:47.300
We think of China as the world’s producer.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:53.600
But the reality is that its ability to feed
even itself is increasingly tenuous at best.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:58.459
And food is only the tip of the (melting)
iceberg.

00:08:58.459 --> 00:09:02.680
Agriculture may account for the majority of
the national water usage, but the resource

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.120
it should be most fearful of is energy.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:13.040
It takes 24 bathtubs of water to produce one
tonne of coal, and yet 86% of its coal is

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:14.839
produced in the North.

00:09:14.839 --> 00:09:20.690
It’s true that China is actively reducing
its reliance on coal, but when it comes to

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:23.570
water, it doesn’t all that much matter.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:30.800
97% of its electricity requires water in one
form or another, and neither oil, gas, nuclear,

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.370
nor hydropower is an effective solution.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:41.649
So while, yes, its reliance is decreasing
over time, it’s just not nearly fast enough.

00:09:41.649 --> 00:09:47.680
And because household use is so minuscule
in comparison, water scarcity forces a trade-off

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:53.430
between energy and food security — neither
of which is palatable.

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:59.149
Nowhere is the situation more dire than around
the capital, Beijing, which makes the Northern

00:09:59.149 --> 00:10:02.959
region as a whole look half serviceable.

00:10:02.959 --> 00:10:09.040
Recall that China has about 2,000 cubic meters
of freshwater per person, overall.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:16.450
Water stress is generally defined as just
slightly less — 1,700 cubic meters per person.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:23.040
Scarcity starts at around 1,000 cubic meters,
and acute scarcity, around 500.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.999
Here are the per capita water resources in
the country’s 10 driest provinces.

00:10:27.999 --> 00:10:35.319
As of 2016, four were already below half the
level of acute scarcity.

00:10:35.319 --> 00:10:42.610
The worst areas are in the same extreme category
as Oman, Pakistan, and Saudi Arabia.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:46.430
These are Chinese government-supplied figures.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:52.100
The Hai River basin, which includes Beijing,
is especially concerning thanks to the stark

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:57.980
contrast between its huge population and scarce
water resources.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:02.779
In 2016, the mayor announced that the city’s
population could not be allowed to grow past

00:11:02.779 --> 00:11:05.570
23 million for this very reason.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.079
It’s simply not sustainable.

00:11:08.079 --> 00:11:12.199
So far we haven’t painted a very pretty
picture.

00:11:12.199 --> 00:11:15.220
But we’ve operated under a foolish assumption.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:21.160
We’ve taken for granted that all water is
good water, which, in China’s case, it most

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.440
definitely is not.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:27.459
The Chinese water quality system has five
levels:

00:11:27.459 --> 00:11:32.750
Level one is nearly drinkable,
Two is lightly polluted but treatable,

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:37.759
Three can be swam or fished in,
Four can be used only for industry and never

00:11:37.759 --> 00:11:42.720
in contact with humans,
And finally, level five is good only for one

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.010
thing: irrigation.

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:48.519
Anything worse is essentially useless.

00:11:48.519 --> 00:11:55.449
Already in 2013, over half of groundwater
around Beijing was level V or worse.

00:11:55.449 --> 00:12:00.050
Just a quarter of the total could even be
treated for human consumption.

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:06.069
These days, the vast majority of groundwater
is level 4 or 5, and nearly one-fifth is worse

00:12:06.069 --> 00:12:08.990
— meaning useless.

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:15.170
Polluted drinking water presents both an economic
and a human cost — disease, cancer, premature

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:18.500
death, and immeasurable hardship.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:23.170
Environmental degradation has caused the possible
extinction of the Yangtze river dolphin and

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:28.519
desertification has led to Beijing sandstorms
so severe that they turn the sky a kind of

00:12:28.519 --> 00:12:34.470
dystopian yellow — that is, if you can see
past the normal pollution.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.879
Given the absence of surface water in the
North, cities there have over-extracted from

00:12:38.879 --> 00:12:43.050
underground aquifers, which carries several
consequences.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:49.319
First, draining groundwater so completely
leaves no backup in times of acute stress,

00:12:49.319 --> 00:12:51.730
like during unusually bad droughts.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:57.410
Second, removing aquifers causes the ground
to collapse, leading to subsidence, which

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:03.610
is when buildings, bridges, and other structures
slowly and unevenly sink into the ground.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:10.760
Shanghai, for instance, has sunk by 2 meters
in the last two decades, and, most embarrassingly,

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:14.680
sections of The Great Wall are visibly eroding.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:21.209
When the question of where to find water arises,
the inevitable answer is always desalination.

00:13:21.209 --> 00:13:28.220
Israel, also starved for water, has effectively
solved its problem with six desalination plants,

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:31.819
which provide the country with about 80% of
its water.

00:13:31.819 --> 00:13:37.360
The difference between it and China, however,
is that the former is tiny and rich.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:42.779
It’s estimated that to desalinate and transfer
one cubic meter of saltwater from the coast

00:13:42.779 --> 00:13:49.800
of China inland would consume another .50-75
cubic meters of water in the process.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:56.610
Desalination is extremely energy intensive,
China’s energy comes mostly from coal, and

00:13:56.610 --> 00:14:00.709
coal uses lots and lots of water.

00:14:00.709 --> 00:14:02.170
Can you spot the problem?

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:08.050
Well then, you might think, the problem is
really only one of allocation.

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:12.749
Water can be moved, so why not even out the
geographic differences?

00:14:12.749 --> 00:14:14.179
Mao agreed.

00:14:14.179 --> 00:14:20.269
In 1952, he observed, very matter-of-factly:
“There's a lot of water in the south, but

00:14:20.269 --> 00:14:21.800
not much in the north.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:25.429
If we could borrow some, then everything would
be OK."

00:14:25.429 --> 00:14:30.949
It took half a century, but Mao’s suggestions
do not go unanswered.

00:14:30.949 --> 00:14:37.019
The equally straightforwardly named South-North
Water Transfer Project began operation in

00:14:37.019 --> 00:14:38.720
2002.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:45.600
The largest in the world, it consists of 2,700
miles of artificial waterways and canals designed

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:49.760
to do one thing: move water North.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:55.259
The Eastern route is a major upgrade to the
ancient Grand Canal, which pumps water from

00:14:55.259 --> 00:14:57.800
the Yangtze up to Tianjin.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:04.319
The Central route, meanwhile, is also carried
under the Yellow River, but by gravity, and

00:15:04.319 --> 00:15:06.980
all the way up to Beijing.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:12.179
A third, Western route is planned to connect
tributaries of the Yangtze to the Yellow River,

00:15:12.179 --> 00:15:16.660
and will be the most technically demanding,
as its path intersects mountains.

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:22.449
There’s no doubt that the project — which
spans four major rivers, three megacities,

00:15:22.449 --> 00:15:26.860
and six provinces — is a true engineering
wonder.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:31.980
One could reasonably argue that only China
has the unique combination of political and

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:38.149
economic infrastructure to relocate entire
towns, coordinate between such disparate levels

00:15:38.149 --> 00:15:43.239
of government, and make the requisite legal
changes at this scale.

00:15:43.239 --> 00:15:47.589
This is not meant to imply that the project
was uncontroversial.

00:15:47.589 --> 00:15:54.910
Expected to cost a massive $62 billion, it
already exceeded that number a decade ago,

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:59.420
and 345,000 people have been relocated in
the process.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:04.730
Some are happy with their compensation, but
others complain their new life is measurably

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:05.730
worse.

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:12.889
The first drop of water, meanwhile, reached
Beijing in 2015, 7 years after the 2008 Summer

00:16:12.889 --> 00:16:15.309
Olympics, the original goal.

00:16:15.309 --> 00:16:21.350
Now, all of these costs would be one thing
if the project singularly solved China’s

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:22.860
water woes.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:26.019
Unfortunately, not even close.

00:16:26.019 --> 00:16:32.889
Even if it supplied only the Beijing/Tianjin/Hebei
area, these places would still be categorized

00:16:32.889 --> 00:16:35.049
as acutely scarce.

00:16:35.049 --> 00:16:39.369
Yet it’s also responsible for three other
provinces.

00:16:39.369 --> 00:16:45.170
Even at its imagined full capacity, it would
only offset one-fourth of Northern China’s

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:51.350
annual demand of 203 billion cubic meters
by 2050, according to the Chinese Institute

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:53.420
of Water Resources.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:59.670
In other words, decades of engineering, forced
relocations, and environmental destruction

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:03.760
all amount to, essentially, a band-aid.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:08.680
Perhaps worse than a band-aid — which may
not heal wounds but at least doesn’t make

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:10.390
them worse.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:15.740
These canals don’t create water out of thin
air, they merely move it — shifting the

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:18.820
problem, quite literally, downstream.

00:17:18.820 --> 00:17:23.360
Southern provinces, worried about their own
supply and quality of water, increasingly

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:28.110
rely themselves on water transfer projects
and dams from elsewhere.

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:32.260
When the music stops, someone will pay the
price.

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:37.200
All of this makes the enormity of the project
sort of ironic.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:41.530
On one hand, it represents an amazing technical
accomplishment.

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:47.150
On the other, it represents the extraordinary
lengths China will go only to paint the superficial

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.550
veneer of progress.

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:54.980
The much more obvious solution is to target
not supply but demand.

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:59.720
The price of water in China is among the lowest
in the world — less than a third of what

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:04.090
it is in the U.S. and just a quarter of the
world average.

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:09.060
Because water is so heavily subsidized, farmers
have little reason to make efficient use of

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:10.060
it.

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:15.280
The most common method of irrigation is flooding,
when farmers aimlessly dump water over their

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:18.770
fields until most evaporates or runs off.

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:22.760
About half of the water used in this process
is wasted.

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:27.910
These inefficiencies are reflected in China’s
low water productivity — which is defined

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:32.830
as the crop value generated for every cubic
meter of water used.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:38.040
Various regional governments have dabbled
in market-based reforms, but not nearly as

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:42.210
much as one would expect, given how much is
at stake.

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:48.710
Price adjustments are not, to be clear, some
magical silver bullet, but they do raise an

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:55.130
important question: Why did China spend a
hundred billion dollars building a water conveyor

00:18:55.130 --> 00:19:00.800
belt from New York to LA, when it could have
just raised prices?

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:06.600
Some experts point the curiously high number
of engineers among China’s top ranks — many

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.240
even specialized in hydraulics.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:14.910
At one point, all of the nine Politburo standing
committee members were engineers.

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:20.110
It’s probably true that hydraulic engineers
see everything as a hydraulic engineering

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:25.300
problem, and it doesn’t hurt that the country
has historically sought high-profile infrastructure

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:28.220
projects as a means of gaining legitimacy.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:29.220
But.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:35.380
There’s another reason — one that has
nothing at all to do with water in particular.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:41.140
The reason China opts for a band-aid to solve
its water crisis is the same reason it waited

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:47.280
decades to halt the One Child Policy, long
past when it's disastrous effects were made

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:53.280
clear, and the same reason it pays only lip
service to the housing crisis.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:20:00.750
There’s one thing China fears far, far more
than a demographic, housing, or even water

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:06.670
crisis, and it also just so happens to be
the key to understanding it’s aggressive

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:12.980
posture — why it seems to pick fights on
the world stage and needlessly harm relations

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:14.680
with other countries.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:20.020
Next time, in part four of China’s Reckoning.

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:24.830
